    "questionText": "An 82-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her daughter for new-onset confusion, agitation, and visual hallucinations that began two days ago. She has no prior psychiatric history. Vital signs are notable for a temperature of 38.1°C. The lecturer specifically named one common, easily diagnosable, and reversible cause of altered mental status in the geriatric population that must be considered before attributing her presentation to a primary psychiatric disorder. Which initial test should be ordered to evaluate this cause?",
